// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row match_array_elem_struct_binder_aliases_source (tier 3 origin, observed `run 1`).
// A BY-VALUE element binder over an array of STRUCTS ALIASES the source array instead of copying it.
//
// `match a { [_, y] => { a[1].v = 50; out = y.v; } }` over `a: [P; 2]`: rustc 1.98.1 --edition 2024
// MEASURED — compiles, runs exit 0, prints got=2 (the binder holds a COPY made when the arm was
// entered). TODAY logosc compiles this clean and prints got=50, exit 1: the write through `a[1]`
// is visible in `y`, so the binder is an ALIAS of the element place.
//
// ⚠ PRE-EXISTING AND REACHABLE ON THE SHIPPED BINARY — NOT caused by the 2026-09-17l match-place
// change. MEASURED on the base binary 46fd328ab/c885cb0d2 (logosc of 2026-09-17 11:31) and on the
// armed binary of the same round: got=50 on BOTH, byte for byte. The local-array spelling below is
// the carrier precisely because it compiles on both, so the observation does not depend on that round.
// What that round DID change is exposure: the same shape spelled through a struct FIELD
// (`match s.arr { [_, y] => … }`) was previously refused by an MLIR verifier error and now reaches
// this defect instead. Its carrier is hand program x11 of round 2026-09-17l.
//
// The door is the element binder, not the match base: bind_elem GEPs the element and hands the
// ADDRESS to pat_bind, whose Wild case binds a struct-typed slot as a PLACE (that is correct for a
// `ref` binder and for a scrutinee that is already a temporary, and wrong for a by-value binder over
// a live place). FIX = the by-value binder copies; `ref`/`ref mut` keep the address.
// FIX ORACLE = compiles AND prints got=2, exit 0.
package match_array_elem_struct_binder_aliases_source;
extern fn printf(fmt: *const u8, ...) -> i32;
struct P { v: i64 }
fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut a: [P; 2] = [P { v: 1i64 }, P { v: 2i64 }];
    let mut out: i64 = 0i64;
    match a {
        [_, y] => { a[1].v = 50i64; out = y.v; }
    }
    unsafe { printf("got=%ld\n".as_ptr(), out); }
    if out != 2i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
